\id LUK - Aperto Bible (English) v5.3.0-inverted (2026-07-02)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Luke
\toc1 The Gospel of Luke
\toc2 Luke
\toc3 Lk
\mt1 Luke

\c 1

\s1 To Theophilus
\p
\v 1-2 Many have already set out to tell the story of what has happened among us, \add events God brought to completion\add*\f + \fr 1:1 \ft TEXT: The Greek perfect tense (πεπληροφορημένων) emphasizes divine fulfillment and completion, not merely historical occurrence.\f*, based on what was handed down to us \add in the way teachers transmit to their students\add*\f + \fr 1:2 \ft KULTUR: In both Jewish and Greco-Roman education, authoritative teaching was formally transmitted through recognized chains from teacher to student.\f* by those who saw it from the beginning and became servants of the word.
\v 3-4 So it seemed right to me as well, having followed everything carefully from the start, to write it all out in order for you, most excellent Theophilus,\add whose support made this possible\add*\f + \fr 1:3 \ft KULTUR: The honorific "most excellent" (κράτιστε) indicates high social standing. Literary works were typically funded by wealthy patrons; dedicating a work honored both writer and recipient.\f*, so that you may know the solid ground beneath what you've been taught.

\s1 An Old Priest and an Impossible Promise
\p
\v 5-7 During the reign of Herod, king of Judea, there was a priest named Zechariah who belonged to the priestly division of Abijah\f + \ft KULTUR: The course of Abijah was the eighth of twenty-four priestly divisions (1 Chronicles 24:10), each serving one week twice yearly plus major festivals (Bovon, Hermeneia; Fitzmyer, Anchor).\f*. His wife Elizabeth was also a descendant of Aaron. Both of them were righteous in God's sight, living blamelessly according to all the commandments and requirements of the \nd Lord\nd*\f + \ft NAME: LORD, in small capitals, represents God's personal name, YHWH, Yahweh.\f*. But they had no children. Elizabeth was barren, and both of them were well along in years.
\v 8-10 One day when Zechariah's division was on duty and he was serving as priest before God, he was chosen by lot, \add the honor he had waited decades for\add*\f + \ft KULTUR: Priestly divisions served one week twice yearly; being chosen by lot to offer incense was a once-in-a-lifetime privilege most priests never received (Bovon, Hermeneia; Bock, Baker).\f*, according to the custom of the priesthood, to enter the sanctuary of the \nd Lord\nd* and burn incense. The hour of incense had come, and outside, the whole assembly was praying.
\v 11-12 Then an angel of the \nd Lord\nd* appeared to him, standing at the right side of the incense altar. When Zechariah saw him, fear overwhelmed him.
\v 13 But the angel said to him, "Don't be afraid, Zechariah. Your prayer has been heard. Your wife Elizabeth will bear you a son, and you are to name him John.
\v 14 He will be a joy and delight to you, and many will rejoice because of his birth.
\v 15 He will be great in the sight of the \nd Lord\nd*. He will never drink wine or other fermented drink, and he will be filled with the Holy Spirit even before he is born.
\v 16 He will bring many of the people of Israel back to the \nd Lord\nd* their God.
\v 17 And he will go before the \nd Lord\nd*, in the spirit and power of Elijah, to turn the hearts of parents to their children and the disobedient to the wisdom of the righteous: to make ready a people prepared for the \nd Lord\nd*."
\v 18 Zechariah asked the angel, "How can I be sure of this? I'm an old man, and my wife is well along in years."
\v 19 The angel answered, "I am Gabriel. I stand in the presence of God, and I've been sent to bring you this good news.
\v 20 Now you'll be silent. You won't be able to speak until the day this happens, because you didn't believe my words, which will come true exactly when they're supposed to."
\v 21-22 Meanwhile, the people were waiting for Zechariah and wondering why he was taking so long in the sanctuary. When he came out, he couldn't speak to them: \add couldn't give them the blessing they were waiting for\add*\f + \ft KULTUR: The priest emerging from the Holy Place would pronounce the Aaronic blessing (Numbers 6:24-26) over the assembly; Zechariah's silence meant this expected benediction could not be given (Bock, Baker).\f*. They realized he had seen a vision, for he kept making signs to them but remained unable to speak.
\v 23-25 When his time of service was over, he went home. After this, his wife Elizabeth became pregnant and for five months stayed in seclusion. "The \nd Lord\nd* has done this for me," she said. "He has shown his favor and taken away my disgrace among the people\f + \ft HARM: Elizabeth's "disgrace" (ὄνειδος, oneidos) was social stigma imposed by others, never divine judgment on her character; the narrative vindicates her as righteous (v. 6) (Bovon, Hermeneia).\f*."

\s1 Mary Says Yes
\p
\v 26-27 When Elizabeth was six months along, God sent the angel Gabriel to Nazareth, a village in Galilee.\add  Rome had never bothered to learn its name. A peasant town beneath anyone's notice, which is exactly where the Most High chose to begin.\add*\f + \fr 1:26-27 \fk KONTEXT \fq Rome had never bothered to learn its name \ft Nazareth was a small agricultural village beneath the notice of Rome and largely of Jerusalem. Luke's reversal theme is already running: the supreme God selects a nobody from nowhere, not a royal house in a capital. \f* He came to a young woman pledged in marriage to a man named Joseph, from David's line. The pledge was legally binding, the same as marriage in everything but living together. Her name was Mary. She had never slept with a man.\add  Everyone in the village would know that a pregnancy now, before Joseph took her into his house, could only mean one thing: betrayal. A betrothed girl found pregnant faced public denunciation, divorce, even death by stoning. Joseph's royal bloodline was no incidental detail: it would carry straight into this child's legal name and inheritance rights, regardless of who fathered him.\add*\f + \fr 1:26-27 \fk KONTEXT \fq The pledge was legally binding ... royal bloodline \ft Betrothal was legally binding, broken only by divorce; the family's honor was at stake. Legal paternity through Joseph conveyed inheritance and lineage rights regardless of biological generation, which is how the child receives the Davidic claim (2 Sam 7). A pregnant betrothed girl faced suspicion of adultery, a capital offense under the Law, though actual executions were rare under Roman rule. Joseph's options included public denunciation or quiet divorce. Mary's yes was socially dangerous.\f*
\v 28-29 The angel came to her and said, "Be glad, favored one.\add  God has poured out his grace on you, and it rests on you still.\add* The Lord is with you."\f + \fr 1:28 \fk TEXT \fq favored one ... it rests on you still \ft "Favored one" renders the perfect passive participle kecharitomene: a completed action with an ongoing result. Mary has been graced and remains so. Origen noted this exact greeting is found nowhere else in Scripture, addressed to Mary alone. \f* The words shook her. She turned them over and over, trying to make sense of a greeting like that.\f + \fr 1:29 \fk TEXT \fq The words shook her \ft The verb dietarachthe denotes deep inner turmoil, not mild surprise, and dielogizeto pictures her reasoning the greeting through, an interior weighing rather than passive confusion. \f*
\v 30-33 "Don't be afraid, Mary," the angel said. "God has chosen to show you his favor. Listen: you are going to conceive and give birth to a son, and you are to name him Jesus. He will be great. He will be called the Son of the Most High, and the Lord God will give him the throne of his ancestor David. He will reign over the people of Jacob forever, and his kingdom will never end."\add  This is the promise sworn to David coming due at last: the throne re-established, now without end.\add*\f + \fr 1:30-33 \fk KONTEXT \fq his kingdom will never end \ft The piled titles—Son of the Most High, throne of David, an everlasting reign—announce the fulfillment of God's covenant promise to David (2 Sam 7). To a first hearer these were not generic honorifics but the long-awaited Messianic claim. \f*
\v 34 "How can this happen," Mary asked, "when I have never slept with a man?"\add  Not whether God could be trusted, but how this would actually work. She knew what a pregnancy would cost her, the shame it would bring, the danger. And still she stayed in the conversation.\add*\f + \fr 1:34 \fk TEXT \fq How can this happen \ft Mary's question is inquiry, not doubt. She asks "how?" not "how can I be sure?" In contrast to Zechariah, her question is met with explanation rather than silencing. The illumination captures the social cost she understood: pregnancy during betrothal created suspicion of adultery, and Mary's question reflects awareness of the social impossibility, not biological ignorance.\f*
\v 35 The angel answered, "The Holy Spirit will come upon you, and the power of the Most High will settle over you,\add  the way God's presence once filled the tent of meeting. The same glory-cloud that overshadowed the tabernacle now overshadows you, and you become the place where God dwells.\add* So the child will be holy, and he will be called the Son of God.\f + \fr 1:35 \fk KONTEXT \fq the way God's presence once filled the tent of meeting \ft "Will settle over you" renders episkiasei, "overshadow," echoing the cloud that covered the tent of meeting so that the glory of the \nd LORD\nd* filled it (Exod 40:35). Mary becomes the new locus of divine presence, and the Spirit, not human agency, is the agent of conception. \f*
\v 36-37 And here is something more: your relative Elizabeth, the one everyone called barren, is six months pregnant in her old age.\add  The proof is already breathing, a sign you can go and see for yourself.\add* No word that comes from God will ever fail."\f + \fr 1:36-37 \fk KONTEXT \fq six months pregnant in her old age \ft Elizabeth's pregnancy is offered as a confirming sign. Faith here is not blind: the impossible has already begun in someone Mary knows, answering the unspoken question of how anyone could believe such news. \f*
\v 38 "I am the \nd LORD\nd*'s servant," Mary said.\add  His to command, by my own choosing and no one else's.\add* "I give myself to this. Let everything happen to me just as you have said."\f + \fr 1:38 \fk TEXT \fq I am the LORD's servant \ft Doule is a female bondservant, but here it is Mary's own self-designation, a freely chosen radical availability to God, not subservience imposed by men. "Let everything happen to me" renders an optative of willing consent: a powerful active yes, her agency, not passive compliance. The text has been weaponized to enforce female subservience; Mary's virtue is her faith-response, not merely physical state.\f* And the angel left her.

\s1 Two Women, Two Miracles
\p
\v 39-40 Mary set out right away and hurried into the hill country of Judea \add eighty miles south, several days on foot\add*\f + \fr 1:39 \fk KONTEXT: \ft The journey from Nazareth to the Judean hill country was approximately 80-100 miles and would have taken several days. Bovon, Hermeneia; archaeological evidence from Ein Karem region.\f*. She came to the town where Zechariah lived, entered his house, and greeted Elizabeth.
\v 41 When Elizabeth heard Mary's greeting, the baby inside her leaped. And Elizabeth, \add older, married to a priest\add*\f + \fr 1:41 \fk KULTUR: \ft In ancient Near Eastern culture, age and priestly family status conferred honor. Elizabeth's deference to the younger Mary inverts normal social patterns. Bovon, Hermeneia; Malina & Rohrbaugh, Social-Science Commentary.\f*, was filled with the Holy Spirit and cried out in a loud voice:
\v 42-45 "Blessed are you among women \add standing with the warriors and deliverers\add*\f + \fr 1:42 \fk KULTUR: \ft The phrase "blessed among women" echoes blessings of Jael (Judges 5:24) and Judith (Judith 13:18), Israel's heroic women who delivered the people. Bovon, Hermeneia.\f*, and blessed is the child you are carrying! Why me? How is it that the mother of my Lord should come to me? The moment I heard your greeting, the baby inside me leaped for joy! And blessed is she who believed that what the Lord said to her would be fulfilled."

\s1 Mary's Song
\v 46
\q1 Everything in me celebrates the \nd LORD\nd*,
\v 47
\q2 my spirit rejoices in God my Savior:
\v 48
\q1 he has looked on me, his servant, \add me with no standing\add*.\f + \fr 1:48 \ft me with no standing: Greek ταπείνωσις (tapeinōsis), referring to Mary's low social position—young, poor, female, colonized. The reversal begins here: the one with no status becomes the mother of the Messiah. [TEXT: BDAG 989; Louw-Nida 87.60; KONTEXT: Bovon, Hermeneia, 63-64]\f*
\q1 From now on, every generation will call me blessed,
\v 49
\q1 because the Mighty One has done great things for me.
\q2 Holy is his name.
\v 50
\q1 His mercy flows to those who fear him,
\q2 generation after generation.
\v 51
\q1 He has shown strength with his arm.
\q2 He has scattered the proud in their scheming hearts.
\v 52
\q1 He has pulled rulers down from their thrones, \add those who grind the faces of the poor\add*,\f + \fr 1:52 \ft those who grind the faces of the poor: Echoes Isaiah 3:15, where God confronts Israel's rulers: "What do you mean by crushing my people, by grinding the face of the poor?" Mary's song stands in this prophetic tradition of judgment on oppressors. [TEXT: Isaiah 3:15; KONTEXT: Bovon, Hermeneia, 65-66]\f*
\q2 and lifted up the nobodies.
\v 53
\q1 He has filled the hungry with good things
\q2 and sent the rich away empty.
\v 54
\q1 He has reached out to Israel, his servant,
\q2 remembering his mercy,
\v 55
\q1 just as he promised our ancestors,
\q2 to Abraham and his descendants forever.
\v 56
\p And Mary stayed with Elizabeth about three months, then returned home.

\s1 His Name Is John
\p
\v 57-58 When the time came, Elizabeth gave birth to a son. Word got out: neighbors, relatives, everyone heard how the Lord had poured mercy on her. They came to celebrate.
\v 59-61 On the eighth day \add they gathered for the circumcision ceremony, when the child would be formally named and entered into the covenant people\add*\f + \fr 1:59 \fk KULTUR \ft Circumcision on the eighth day (Leviticus 12:3) was the sign of covenant membership; naming occurred during this ceremony. Source: Fitzmyer, *Anchor*, 385.\f* they all assumed he'd be called Zechariah, after his father. \add Naming a son after his father or grandfather honored the family line. Everyone expected it.\add*\f + \fr 1:59 \fk KONTEXT \ft First-century Jewish families typically named sons after male relatives to preserve family identity and honor. Source: Bovon, *Hermeneia*, 72.\f*
But Elizabeth cut in. "No. His name is John."
They looked at her, confused. "John? There's no one in your family with that name."
\v 62-63 So they turned to Zechariah, using signs, since he still couldn't speak, asking what *he* wanted to call the child.
He motioned for a writing tablet.\f + \fr 1:63 \fk KONTEXT \ft A πινακίδιον (pinakidion) was a small wooden tablet with a wax surface for temporary writing. Source: BDAG 813.\f* Everyone watched as he wrote: "His name IS John." \add The name means "YHWH has shown grace," a summary of everything that had happened.\add*\f + \fr 1:63 \fk NAME \ft Greek Ἰωάννης (Iōannēs), from Hebrew יוֹחָנָן (Yohanan), "YHWH has shown grace" or "YHWH is gracious." Source: BDAG 465; Fitzmyer, *Anchor*, 386.\f*
They were stunned.
\v 64 And right then, immediately, Zechariah's mouth opened. His tongue loosened. The first words out: blessing God.
\v 65-66 Fear came over everyone in the neighborhood. The news spread through the entire Judean hill country. People couldn't stop talking about it. Everyone who heard asked the same question: "What is this child going to become?" 
Because clearly, the hand of \nd the LORD\nd*\f + \fr 1:66 \fk NAME \ft LORD in small capitals represents the divine name YHWH (Yahweh).\f*,\add the power and favor of God himself,\add*\f + \fr 1:66 \fk TEXT \ft "Hand of the LORD" (χεὶρ κυρίου) is an Old Testament idiom for divine power, presence, and protection (cf. Exodus 9:3; 1 Samuel 5:6; Isaiah 59:1). Source: BDAG 1082; Louw-Nida 76.4.\f* was with him.

\s1 Zechariah's Prophecy
\q1 \v 67 And his father Zechariah, flooded with the Holy Spirit, began to prophesy,\add  the silenced priest, his tongue loosed at last, now speaking for God:\add*\f + \fr 1:67 \fk KONTEXT \fq began to prophesy \ft Zechariah has been mute since the angel's word in the temple. His first full speech is not small talk but Spirit-filled prophecy, the elderly priest who could not speak now opening with words that belong to the great prophets before him.\f*
\q1 \v 68 Praise the \nd LORD\nd*, the God of Israel,\add  blessed be he, in the words our prayers have always begun,\add*\f + \fr 1:68 \fk KULTUR \fq Praise the LORD, the God of Israel \ft This is the opening of a berakah, the blessing-form every Jewish prayer used (compare 1 Kings 1:48; Psalm 41:13). A worshipper in the synagogue would recognize the cadence at once: Zechariah, the priest, is praying in the very shape of his liturgical world. LORD in small capitals represents God's personal name, YHWH, Yahweh.\f*
\q2 for he has come to his people and bought them back.\add  He has visited us at last, paying the ransom to set his people free, as he once bought us out of Egypt.\add*\f + \fr 1:68 \fk TEXT \fq he has come to his people and bought them back \ft Two loaded words sit here. "Visited" (episkeptomai) is what God does when he finally steps into his people's plight to act. "Bought back" (lytrosis) is ransom language, the price paid to free a captive, the same word that carries the memory of the Exodus, when God redeemed slaves and made them his own.\f*
\q1 \v 69 He has raised up a mighty Savior for us,\add  a horn of rescue, strength lifted high,\add*\f + \fr 1:69 \fk TEXT \fq a mighty Savior, a horn of rescue \ft The image is literally a "horn of salvation." A horn is the raised strength of a charging animal, and also the horn of oil used to anoint a king. To raise a horn is to raise up power that saves, and to raise it "in the house of David" is to name the long-awaited king from David's line.\f*
\q2 in the house of his servant David,\add  the royal line we have waited generations to see restored.\add*\f + \fr 1:69 \fk KONTEXT \fq in the house of his servant David \ft Israel's hope had long centered on a descendant of David who would restore the kingdom. Under Roman occupation that hope was electric. Naming David's house is naming the promised king, even as the song will soon redirect what kind of king he is.\f*
\q1 \v 70 just as he promised long ago
\q2 through the mouths of his holy prophets:
\q1 \v 71 rescue from our enemies,
\q2 out of the grip of everyone who hates us.\add  Deliverance from the powers that hold us down,\add*\f + \fr 1:71 \fk KONTEXT \fq our enemies, everyone who hates us \ft For a people living under Roman occupation, "enemies" had an obvious face. The hope was real liberation from foreign domination. Yet the song will not end in conquest: its final word is peace, and Luke quietly reshapes the dream of victory into something deeper than driving out an army.\f*
\q1 \v 72 He is keeping faith with our ancestors,
\q2 remembering his holy covenant,
\q1 \v 73 the oath he swore to Abraham our father:\add  the promise that makes us one people, bound to him by ancestry, not saved one by one but together,\add*\f + \fr 1:73 \fk KULTUR \fq the oath he swore to Abraham our father \ft Belonging to God ran through descent from Abraham; the covenant was a family bond, not a private contract. Zechariah is singing of corporate rescue, the whole people redeemed at once because of a promise sworn to their common father.\f*
\q1 \v 74 to pull us free from the hands of our enemies
\q2 so we can serve him unafraid,\add  free to stand before him in worship, the fear gone,\add*\f + \fr 1:74 \fk TEXT \fq so we can serve him unafraid \ft The word for "serve" (latreuo) is the language of priestly worship at the temple. The goal of rescue is not merely safety but unhindered, unafraid worship, exactly the world Zechariah the priest has spent his life inside.\f*
\q1 \v 75 holy and right before him
\q2 every day we live.
\q1 \v 76 And you, little one, you will be called a prophet of the Most High,\add  you, a priest's son, marked out not for the altar but for the road ahead of one greater,\add*\f + \fr 1:76 \fk KONTEXT \fq And you, little one, a prophet of the Most High \ft The song turns from Israel's king to Zechariah's own infant. As a priest's son, John would have been expected to follow his father into temple service. Instead he is named a prophet who runs ahead of the Lord, the Levite's child pointing beyond himself to the greater one from David's line.\f*
\q2 for you will walk out ahead of the Lord to clear his road,
\q1 \v 77 to let his people know they are saved
\q2 because their sins are forgiven.
\q1 \v 78 This is the gut-deep mercy of our God,\add  mercy that turns him over inside, the way a parent aches for a child,\add*\f + \fr 1:78 \fk TEXT \fq the gut-deep mercy of our God \ft The phrase is literally "bowels of mercy." The ancients located deep feeling in the gut, so this names compassion that is visceral, not polite, the kind that moves a person from the inside. From mercy this deep comes everything that follows.\f*
\q2 the mercy by which the Dawn from on high will break over us,\add  the Rising One, dayspring and promised Branch from David's stock, coming up like the sun,\add*\f + \fr 1:78 \fk TEXT \fq the Dawn from on high \ft One Greek word (anatole) holds two pictures at once: the rising of the sun, and the messianic "Branch" sprouting from David's line (Jeremiah 23:5; Zechariah 3:8; 6:12; the "sun of righteousness" of Malachi 4:2). Zechariah's hearers caught both, the dawn that ends the night and the king who springs from the old root.\f*
\q1 \v 79 rising on all who sit in darkness, in the shadow of death,\add  light reaching those left in the dark, the oppressed, the poor, the forgotten,\add*\f + \fr 1:79 \fk KONTEXT \fq all who sit in darkness, in the shadow of death \ft This is the language of Isaiah 9:2, light dawning on a people in darkness, and of Psalm 23:4, the valley of death's shadow. "Those who sit in darkness" are not only the spiritually lost but the crushed and overlooked of an occupied land. The dawn comes first to them.\f*
\q2 to set our feet on the road that leads to peace.\add  Not the peace of a won war but shalom, the whole world set right.\add*\f + \fr 1:79 \fk TEXT \fq the road that leads to peace \ft The final word is peace (eirene), carrying the Hebrew shalom: not merely the absence of fighting but wholeness, everything made right. After all the talk of enemies, the path leads not to violence but here, feet guided into shalom.\f*

\s1 Growing Up in the Wilderness
\p
\v 80 The child grew up, and his spirit grew strong. He lived out in the wilderness \add where prophets are formed\add*\f + \fr 1:80 \ft Moses, Elijah, and Israel itself were all formed in wilderness places before their public ministries. (KULTUR: Fitzmyer, Bock)\f* until the day he appeared publicly to Israel.
